NAME
       mousepad - simple text editor

SYNOPSIS
       mousepad [OPTION...] [FILE...]

DESCRIPTION
       Mousepad  is  a  text editor for quickly editing text files: an editor,
       not a development environment and not a plugin host.  It is the graphi-
       cal counterpart of nano(1) on this system, and opens from the file man-
       ager for any file whose type is text.

       The editing area is a GtkSourceView widget, which is where most of what
       the program can do comes from: syntax highlighting for  some  170  lan-
       guages  chosen  from  the Document -> Filetype menu or guessed from the
       file's name and contents, line numbers, automatic indentation,  bracket
       matching, a whitespace display, and unlimited undo.

       Documents  open as tabs in one window.  A tab can be dragged out into a
       window of its own or dropped onto another window, and Document -> Split
       View shows one document twice so that two places in a long file can  be
       edited at once.

       Text  is  read and written in the encoding named by --encoding; without
       one Mousepad reads UTF-8 and falls back to the locale's encoding, open-
       ing a dialog when a file is neither.  Line  endings  are  preserved  as
       found and can be changed from Document -> Line Ending.

       Search  and  replace covers the open document or every open document at
       once, with plain text, whole-word or regular-expression  matching,  and
       an incremental search bar bound to Ctrl+F.

OPTIONS
       -e ENCODING, --encoding=ENCODING
              Open the files that follow in ENCODING.  With an empty argument,
              open the encoding dialog for each file instead.

       --list-encodings
              Print  the  encodings this build accepts as an argument to --en-
              coding, and exit.

       -l LINE, --line=LINE
              Put the cursor on line LINE.  A positive number counts from  the
              top of the file, a negative one from the bottom.

       -c COLUMN, --column=COLUMN
              Put  the  cursor in column COLUMN.  Zero or more counts from the
              start of the line, a negative number from its end.

       --preferences
              Open the preferences dialog rather than a document window.

       --disable-server
              Do not become, or talk to, the primary instance: this invocation
              gets its own process and its own windows.  Without it, a  second
              mousepad hands its file names to the one already running and ex-
              its, so that every document shares one window.

       -q, --quit
              Ask  a  running  Mousepad to close.  Unsaved documents are still
              asked about.

       -v, --version
              Print the version and exit.

       The usual GTK options (--display, --class, --name, and  the  rest)  are
       accepted as well; see --help-all for the whole list.

FILES
       ~/.config/Mousepad/settings.conf
              Every  preference  the  program  remembers -- window size, font,
              word wrap, tab width, the  toolbar  and  statusbar,  the  colour
              scheme.

              This  file  is  particular  to this system.  Mousepad stores its
              settings through GSettings, which normally writes them into  the
              dconf  database  over  a  session  bus,  and there is neither on
              LikeOS.  It is therefore built with the keyfile  backend,  which
              is upstream's option for exactly that situation and which writes
              this plain, editable file instead.

       /usr/share/glib-2.0/schemas/
              The  compiled  schema  describing those settings.  Mousepad will
              not start without it.

       /usr/share/gtksourceview-4/language-specs/
              The syntax definitions, one XML file per language, and where  to
              add a language that is not there.

       /usr/share/gtksourceview-4/styles/
              The colour schemes offered under View -> Color Scheme.

       /usr/lib/mousepad/plugins/
              Where  plugin  modules  are loaded from.  None is built for this
              image.

ENVIRONMENT
       GSETTINGS_BACKEND
              Overrides the settings backend described under  FILES.   Setting
              it to memory makes a session's changes deliberately temporary.

EXAMPLES
       Open a file with the cursor on its last line:

              mousepad -l -1 /var/log/messages

       Open a file that is not UTF-8:

              mousepad --encoding=ISO-8859-15 notes.txt

SEE ALSO
       nano(1), pcmanfm(1)

